    "Instructions": {
        "Text": "Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you are instructed as follows:\n\nThis is a civil negligence case. The plaintiff, Olivia Smith, claims the defendant, Marcus Carter, was negligent when he entered an intersection on a yellow signal and collided with her vehicle. To recover damages, the plaintiff must prove by a preponderance of the evidence that: \n\n1. The defendant owed a duty of care to the plaintiff. \n2. The defendant breached that duty by failing to exercise reasonable care. \n3. The defendant's breach proximately caused the plaintiff's injuries. \n4. The plaintiff suffered actual damages as a result. \n\nNorth Carolina uses a modified comparative negligence rule. If you find that the plaintiff was also negligent, you must assign a percentage of fault to each party. The plaintiff may recover only if her percentage of fault is 75% or less. Any damages award must be reduced by the plaintiff's percentage of fault. \n\nIf the plaintiff's fault is greater than 75%, she may not recover at all. \n\nYou are the sole judges of the credibility of witnesses and the weight of the evidence. Your verdict must be based only on the evidence presented in this trial and these instructions of law.",
        "Standard": "PreponderanceOfEvidence"
    },
